Nitrogen addition increases the fraction of LAGBs while reducing both the KAM and FWHM. This apparent contradiction is highly correlated with a sequence of defect‑mediated accommodation processes. Among these, the solid solution of nitrogen induces lattice distortion, generating dislocations and microstrain. Once the dislocation density reaches a critical level, the dynamic recovery promotes their rearrangement into transverse walls via partial annihilation and polygonization, forming distinct subgrains within α-laths (Fig.  11 a) [ 88 ]. This cooperative mechanism constitutes a distinct “Solid Solution + Recovery” pathway. To quantitatively substantiate this pathway, a dual-scale evaluation of the dislocation density was conducted (as shown in Fig.  S6 ). Based on the Williamson-Hall (W-H) method, the bulk microstrain ( ε ) drastically decreased from 0.00439 for the pure Ar specimen to 0.00188 for the 10% N 2  specimen. Consequently, the macroscopic total dislocation density ( ρ Total ) dropped significantly from 3.19 × 10 15  to 7.79 × 10 14  and to 5.85 × 10 14  m − 2  [ 89 ]. This severe reduction mathematically confirms the massive annihilation of Statistically Stored Dislocations (SSDs) driven by the intense in-situ thermal recovery. In striking contrast, the Geometrically Necessary Dislocation (GND) density ( ρ GND ) mathematically derived from the local misorientation maps maintained a highly stable plateau (9.08 × 10 14  − 8.70 × 10 14  − 8.84 × 10 14  m − 2 ) across all conditions. Since  ρ Total  comprises both GNDs and SSDs, this decoupling strictly indicates that while random SSD tangles vanished, the geometrically required dislocations survived by dynamically rearranging into the stable LAGB networks [ 90 ]. Misorientation profiles across these subgrains show a step‑like increase at LAGBs, in contrast to the gradual gradient in dislocation‑rich regions (Fig.  11 b), confirming polygonization and local strain relaxation. Additionally, nitrogen significantly promotes the formation of deformation twinning. EBSD maps reveal a marked rise in boundaries with ∼64° and ∼85° misorientations, corresponding to the  \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
